MMBasic for MacOS (MMB4M)
=========================

An overview of what covers what. This file is not itself a licence, and where it
and a licence text disagree, the licence text wins.

MMB4M as a whole is covered by MMBasic's own modified BSD license, five numbered
conditions, in licenses/MMB4L/LICENSE.MMBasic. Some source files are dual-licensed with the MIT
License in licenses/MMB4L/LICENSE.MIT, and say so in their header comments.

Those two are MMB4L's own licence files, reproduced word for word as their terms
require, which is why they are headed "MMBasic for Linux". Other third-party code
in src/ carries its own attributions in the file headers.


Copyright
---------

  Copyright 2011-2026 Geoff Graham
  Copyright 2016-2026 Peter Mather
  Copyright 2021-2026 Thomas Hugo Williams
  Copyright 2026 Thomas Schimana

The interpreter is MMBasic by Geoff Graham and Peter Mather, ported to Linux by
Thomas Hugo Williams as MMB4L, https://github.com/thwill1000/mmb4l. MMB4L was
derived with permission from MMBasic for DOS and takes code and ideas from the
PicoMite, PicoMite VGA, Colour Maximite 2 and Windows versions.

The last line above is this port's, and it is an addition to the startup banner
rather than a replacement, which clause 4 of licenses/MMB4L/LICENSE.MMBasic
allows. The original message stays. What this port changes is the macOS build
target, PATH_MAX from the macOS header, the baud rates macOS termios does not
define, the default editor, and that banner line.

SDL2, https://libsdl.org, is compiled into the released binaries and is covered by
the zlib licence.


Two clauses that matter to a port
---------------------------------

Clause 4: the name "MMBasic" is used when referring to the interpreter, and the
original copyright message is displayed on the console at startup. These two are
also the conditions under which Geoff Graham gave permission for this port, in
correspondence of 2026-08-25.

Clause 5: advertising material mentioning features or use of this software must
acknowledge that it includes software developed by Geoff Graham, Peter Mather and
Thomas Hugo Williams.
